<BaseLayout
  title="What affects your premium"
  description="Driver, vehicle, property, and location factors that commonly affect car and home insurance premiums."
>
  <a class="back-link" href="/learn/">&larr; All guides</a>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>What affects your premium</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      No single factor sets your price. Insurers combine many inputs, and
      which ones they're allowed to use — and how much weight each gets — is
      set by state law and each insurer's own filed rating plan. The lists
      below describe common factors, not a formula.
    </p>

    <h2>Auto insurance</h2>

    <h3>Driver age and experience</h3>
    <p>
      Age can be one factor among many where state law allows it. Teen and
      young drivers are often priced differently than experienced drivers,
      but the actual effect depends on the state, the insurer, and the
      driver's own record — age alone doesn't determine price.
    </p>

    <h3>Driving history</h3>
    <p>
      A clean record, a speeding ticket, an at-fault accident, a comprehensive
      or collision claim, or a DUI/DWI can each be treated differently by
      different insurers. None of these guarantee a specific increase or
      decrease — insurers weigh recency, severity, and your overall record
      together.
    </p>

    <h3>Vehicle characteristics</h3>
    <p>
      Two similarly priced vehicles can carry very different insurance costs.
      Relevant characteristics include the vehicle's value and repair cost,
      how often that model is stolen, its safety rating and safety equipment,
      and how expensive its parts are to source. This is about the insurer's
      claims and repair experience with that vehicle, not just its sticker
      price.
    </p>

    <h3>Location</h3>
    <p>
      Your state, and often your ZIP code, affects pricing through traffic
      density, accident frequency, vehicle theft rates, weather risk, and the
      local litigation environment. Insurance rules and required minimums
      also vary by state — see the
      <a href="/auto-insurance/">verified auto insurance rules by state</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Home insurance</h2>

    <h3>Property characteristics</h3>
    <p>
      Construction type, age of the home, roof age and condition, square
      footage, and replacement cost all factor into pricing, because they
      affect both the likelihood and the cost of a claim.
    </p>

    <h3>Location and disaster exposure</h3>
    <p>
      Wildfire, hurricane, tornado, hail, and flood exposure vary sharply by
      location, and homeowners insurance generally does not cover every
      disaster automatically — some, like flood and earthquake, typically
      require separate coverage. See
      <a href="/learn/coverage-types/">coverage types</a> for what's usually
      included and what isn't.
    </p>

    <h3>Claims history and deductible</h3>
    <p>
      A home's prior claims history and the deductible you choose both affect
      price. A higher deductible generally lowers the premium in exchange for
      paying more out of pocket if you file a claim — see
      <a href="/learn/deductibles/">how deductibles trade off against premium</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        State law can restrict which rating factors an insurer may use at
        all — for example, some states limit or prohibit using credit-based
        scoring for certain lines. Only rely on rules we've verified from a
        primary source: see the
        <a href="/auto-insurance/">auto</a> and
        <a href="/home-insurance/">home</a> state pages. If a state isn't
        listed there yet, that rule hasn't been verified.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
